Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:08.650 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  Charleston has a complex history because  of its ties with the Atlantic slave  trade in recent years historic sites  have reevaluated how they tell the city  story CBS this morning Saturday co-host  Michelle Miller shows us the changes  Charleston's history certainly  complicated for the last seven years  Charleston has been the top US city for  travel and leisure readers a city known  for its cobblestone streets in elegant  homes but just 200 years ago the city  was also the capital of the u.s. slave  trade nearly 40% of all enslaved  Africans brought here came through the  city's port a harsh reality than many in  Charleston used to gloss over I think  it's crucial that we tell the entire  story now tour guides like John Laverne  make it the most important point to  visitors because a lot of people what  they know about Charleston is what  they've seen on TV you've got to discuss  every horrific part of the entire  institution of slavery but norm powers  heads up the Center for the Study of  slavery in Charleston at the College of  Charleston it's very important to be  frank and honest and forthright about  the brutality of the system of slavery I  mean there is there's no getting around  this was an aspect of daily life and  that's exactly what historians at  Charleston's Nathaniel Russell House are  trying to do every house that was built  before 1865 in Charleston was built on  the backs of enslaved men women and  children Seren Northup is the director  of museums for the Historic Charleston  foundation it's so important that  visitors to these sites understand that  enslaved people were people they had  agency they had skills they had hopes  and dreams and relationships and  families and while some say Charleston  is making progress others believe the  work is just beginning the disparities  that exist between african-americans and  whites in this community they are still  very glaring otha meadows president and  CEO of the Charleston Triton Urban  League says those inequalities could  turn black voters and  Charleston away from the polls in 2020  how many folk in the african-american  community actually go out and exercise  their right to vote I think a lot of  African Americans don't feel that  elected officials are making policy and  decisions in their best interests they  are disenfranchised the city of  Charleston has been working to bridge  the gap in 2018 the City Council  formally passed a resolution to  apologise for his role in slavery by a  vote of 7 to 5 it was a win some  celebrated as a sign of how far  Charleston has come for others like  councilman William Dudley Gregory the  close margin represented how far  Charleston still has to go we as a  modern day council at a minimum should  recognize the errors of the ways of our  predecessors an attempt to make things  whole while we can recognition will that  work continues at the site where so many  enslaved Africans actually entered the  u.s. constructions underway on the  International African American Museum  which will be dedicated to telling the  stories of Africans is set to be open  next year this is so important because  history is sometimes painful and a lot  of times you know people tried to act  like that didn't happen but I like what  Lauren or Thor said enslaved people were  people with hopes and dreams absolutely  sometimes you recognize the errors of  our way and recognize that yes that  happened you forget it and how can you  gloss over it that's why it's important  that you did this story Thank You  Michelle  we'll be right back

